    title: "韓日學者召開 6G 安全研討會：探討 AI、量子加密與虛假基地台的綜合防禦策略",
    summary: "本次「韓國-日本安全研討會 2026 夏季」由韓國資訊安全學會 6G 安全研究會主辦，匯集了韓國電子通信研究院（ETRI）、國民大學、KT，以及日本情報通信研究機構（NICT）、橫濱國立大學等權威機構的專家。研討會旨在分享韓日兩國 5G/6G 移動通信安全技術，並尋求共同研究機會。各場次重點涵蓋了 6G 網路安全、AI 驅動安全、量子抗性加密（PQC）及虛假基地台（False Base Station）應對等前瞻議題。專家們強調，隨著技術從 5G 邁向 6G，安全威脅並不會消失，而是會持續演變，需要從單一技術應用轉向涵蓋終端、網路、服務、數據的綜合防禦體系。具體技術討論包括利用適應性 AI-IDS 確保網路切片韌性、結合 AI 與量子計算的整合安全策略，以及開發基於 MODI 和 3GPP 標準的虛假基地台偵測技術。與會者共識指出，未來需透過學術界、研究機構和電信業者間的持續合作，將基礎研究轉化為實際網路實證，實現「安全內建的 6G」（Security-by-Design for 6G）。",
    tags: ["6G 安全", "AI-IDS", "量子抗性加密", "虛假基地台", "ETRI", "NICT", "5G 安全"],
    title_en: "Korean and Japanese Scholars Convene 6G Security Seminar: Exploring Comprehensive Defense Strategies for AI, Quantum Cryptography, and False Base Stations",
    summary_en: "The 'Korea-Japan Security Seminar 2026 Summer' was organized by the Korea Institute of Information Security's 6G Security Research Group, gathering experts from authoritative institutions including the Electronics and Telecommunications Research Institute (ETRI), Kookmin University, KT, and Japan's National Institute of Information and Communications Technology (NICT), and Yokohama National University. The seminar aimed to share South Korea and Japan's 5G/6G mobile communication security technologies and seek joint research opportunities. Key sessions covered forward-looking topics such as 6G network security, AI-driven security, Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC), and countermeasures against False Base Stations. Experts emphasized that as technology advances from 5G to 6G, security threats will not disappear but will continue to evolve, necessitating a shift from single-technology applications to a comprehensive defense system covering endpoints, networks, services, and data. Specific technical discussions included utilizing adaptive AI-IDS to ensure network slicing resilience, integrated security strategies combining AI and quantum computing, and developing False Base Station detection techniques based on MODI and 3GPP standards. Attendees reached a consensus that continuous collaboration among academia, research institutions, and telecommunications operators is required to translate fundamental research into practical network implementations, achieving 'Security-by-Design for 6G'.",
